# {{PROJECT_NAME}} agent context hub

This is an **agent-only** shared context hub for every coding agent working in this workspace.
The human operator does not read it directly. Agents translate internal IDs and evidence into
complete, unambiguous explanations in chat.

## Routes — load by intent, never by reading a folder

Opening a feature folder and reading everything is what overloads a session. Ask for the intent you
actually have, and read only what comes back.

| Intent | Command |
|---|---|
| Continue a feature | `node .agents/_tools/hub.mjs route <feature> --intent resume` **and** `hub.mjs status <feature>` |
| Understand the business rules | `hub.mjs route <feature> --intent business` |
| Find the code behind a surface | `hub.mjs route <feature> --intent implementation` |
| Prepare a release | `hub.mjs route <feature> --intent release` |
| Run, watch or repair it | `hub.mjs route <feature> --intent operations` |
| See every feature and its state | `plans/_REGISTRY.md` (generated — never hand-edit) |
| Recall a durable lesson | `memories/README.md` |
| Understand a repository | `context/<repo>/README.md` |

A route returns **prose only**. Delivery evidence — which MR, merged where, deployed, QC'd — lives
only in `feature.yml`: `hub.mjs status <feature>` prints the states, `status --json` adds the MR ids,
branches and commits behind them. Reading a route is never enough to know where the work stands.

`hub.mjs compare <feature>` reports whether the code moved since the documents were last verified.

## Writing back

Each document answers ONE question. Write what you learned into the document that owns it — see
`plans/_INSTRUCTION.md` §"Where to write" — never into whichever file you had open, and never the
same fact twice. Facts that disagree across two documents are worse than a fact recorded nowhere.

After changing plans, tooling or skills:

```bash
node .agents/_tools/hub.mjs doctor
node .agents/_tools/hub.mjs registry check
node .agents/_tools/graph.mjs build && node .agents/_tools/graph.mjs broken
```

## Ownership

| Path | Ownership |
|---|---|
| `context/` | Stable workspace and repository facts, loaded on demand |
| `plans/` | Feature context, delivery state, evidence, and production readiness |
| `memories/` | Durable verified lessons |
| `docs/` | External document registry and project-owned adapters |
| `environments/` | Cross-environment capability and resource comparisons |
| `skills/` | Canonical project/workspace skills |
| `scripts/` | Project/workspace operational scripts |
| `_config/` | Secret-free hub and runtime declarations |
| `_tools/` | Portable deterministic tools governed by `letgo` |

Nothing else belongs at the top level. A regenerable index or cache is not hub context — keep it
outside `.agents/`.

## Invariants

- `letgo` owns the reusable frame and validation contract only.
- This hub is shared by every agent in the workspace.
- `_config/`, `scripts/`, and `skills/` contain project/workspace-specific extensions.
- Agent-owned prose is concise English. Preserve external quotations and exact domain labels.
- An internal identifier is paired with its description outside its defining table.
- Git merge, environment deployment, runtime verification, and QC are separate facts.
